    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(x-5)^{2}=9 \\\\\n&x-5=\\pm3 \\\\\n&x_{1}=8,x_{2}=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe sum of the roots is: $8+2=\\boxed{10}$. Solving the problem, we can expand the equation as follows: $(x - 5)^2 = 9$ can be rewritten as $x^2 - 10x + 25 = 9$. This simplifies to $x^2 - 10x + 16 = 0$. By the properties of quadratic equations, the sum of the roots of the equation $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ is given by $-b/a$. The quadratic equation $x^2 - 10x + 16 = 0$ has a coefficient $a = 1$ and $b = -10$. Therefore, the sum of the roots is $-(-10)/1 = 10$. So, the sum of the roots of the equation $(x - 5)^2 = 9$ is $\\boxed{10}$.",
